---
import League from '../layouts/League.astro';
---
<League title="Accessibility statement" description="How this site was tested for accessibility, with the results." eyebrow="For everyone" heading="Accessibility statement" lead="How this site was tested, with the results.">
  <div class="wrap body prose" style="max-width:860px">
    <p>This site aims to meet WCAG 2.2 level AA. It is a concept project and the league is fictional, but the tests below were run on this site.</p>

    <h2>Test results, October 28, 2026</h2>
    <table>
      <caption>Automated and scripted checks, run after the October 28 redesign</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Check</th><th scope="col">Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">axe-core (WCAG 2.0 and 2.1 A and AA, plus best practices)</th><td>0 violations on all 11 pages, at phone and desktop sizes</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Lighthouse accessibility (5 pages: home, schedule, standings, teams, fields, phone profile)</th><td>100 on every page tested</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Lighthouse performance</th><td>99 to 100; the schedule and standings pages are the heaviest because they load the filter code</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Lighthouse best practices</th><td>100 on every page tested</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Keyboard</th><td>Skip link is the first stop and every stop shows a visible focus ring</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Reflow at 320 and 640 pixels wide</th><td>No horizontal scrolling</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Text spacing</th><td>No clipped text</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Structure</th><td>One h1 per page, no skipped heading levels, every page has a title and description, every image has an alt attribute (empty for decoration)</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Page weight</th><td>About 80 KB or less for any page, including images and the schedule filter code</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Schedule and standings</h2>
    <p>The schedule and standings are real tables with column and row headers and a caption. The division, team and game filters have labels, and the number of games shown is announced to screen readers when you change a filter. On narrow screens the schedule stacks into labelled rows. Calendar files (.ics) are available for each division.</p>

    <h2>What was fixed during testing</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The large division letters on the leader cards failed the contrast check. They are now purely decorative, drawn in the stylesheet, and not part of the page text.</li>
      <li>The Sponsors and Contact pages skipped a heading level. Both now step down in order.</li>
      <li>Team badges are decorative and hidden from screen readers, because every team name is written out beside its badge.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Not yet tested</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Manual screen reader testing (VoiceOver, NVDA), including the filter announcements. This statement will be updated when it is done.</li>
      <li>The checks above load each page once. They do not click through every filter combination.</li>
      <li>Lighthouse "SEO" shows 60, not 100, because the site is deliberately marked <code>noindex</code> so search engines skip this concept project.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Tools: axe-core, Lighthouse 12, and Nest Co.'s own scripted checks. Team names, coaches, scores and sponsors are all invented.</p>
  </div>
</League>
